1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,420
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:08,635
Time to grade this morning's call — the part most shows skip.

3
00:00:08,635 --> 00:00:18,553
UnitedHealth Group Incorporated Common Stock (DE), ticker UNH triggered, then faded to -1.8 percent below the entry.

4
00:00:18,553 --> 00:00:23,512
If it closes a week below the stop, the exit is automatic.

5
00:00:23,512 --> 00:00:26,932
That is not a failure; that is the plan.

6
00:00:26,932 --> 00:00:31,868
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

7
00:00:31,868 --> 00:00:37,277
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

8
00:00:37,242 --> 00:00:40,605
On the day, the book is up 87 dollars.

9
00:00:40,605 --> 00:00:44,233
ConocoPhillips (COP) leads, +3.4 percent.

10
00:00:44,233 --> 00:00:46,622
Dropbox (DBX) lags at -2.7.

11
00:00:46,622 --> 00:00:51,400
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

12
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:54,927
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

13
00:00:54,883 --> 00:01:02,036
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

14
00:01:02,036 --> 00:01:09,037
12 positions, led by Microsoft, ticker MSFT, Eli Lilly and, ticker LLY, MetLife, ticker MET.

15
00:01:09,037 --> 00:01:19,386
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

16
00:01:19,357 --> 00:01:24,139
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

17
00:01:24,139 --> 00:01:26,531
Every morning OROS proposes.

18
00:01:26,531 --> 00:01:37,035
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

19
00:01:37,035 --> 00:01:40,963
Inside the book today: 8 positions up, 4 down.

20
00:01:40,963 --> 00:01:43,269
Best, COP at +3.37 percent.

21
00:01:43,269 --> 00:01:44,977
Worst, DBX at -2.65.

22
00:01:44,977 --> 00:01:49,930
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

23
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:57,104
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -3.16% per trade over 1174 graded positions.

24
00:01:57,104 --> 00:02:04,107
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

25
00:02:04,107 --> 00:02:09,231
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

26
00:02:09,231 --> 00:02:21,528
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 152 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

27
00:02:21,493 --> 00:02:23,747
Over to the sports desk.

28
00:02:23,747 --> 00:02:33,045
Day 75 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

29
00:02:33,045 --> 00:02:39,338
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 104,118.

30
00:02:39,338 --> 00:02:44,879
Then the index fund, in gray, 2.3 thousand back at 101,846.

31
00:02:44,879 --> 00:02:52,111
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 7.7 thousand back at 96,391.

32
00:02:52,111 --> 00:02:54,740
The race has no finish line.

33
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:59,061
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

34
00:02:59,061 --> 00:03:03,944
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

35
00:03:03,944 --> 00:03:05,823
Nothing is restated.

36
00:03:05,823 --> 00:03:14,731
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

37
00:03:14,693 --> 00:03:22,028
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

38
00:03:22,028 --> 00:03:24,269
Nothing here is financial advice.

39
00:03:24,269 --> 00:03:27,258
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
